{"causes": ["The political ambition of Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, to expand Burgundian influence into the Prince-Bishopric of Liège directly led to Louis's controversial appointment.", "Louis de Bourbon's prestigious noble birth and familial ties to the French royal house and the House of Burgundy provided the necessary leverage for his ecclesiastical advancement.", "Pre-existing tensions between the independent-minded citizenry of Liège and the traditional authority of their Prince-Bishops created fertile ground for conflict upon Louis's imposed rule."], "effects": ["Louis de Bourbon's controversial rule and reliance on Burgundian military support led to repeated uprisings and the devastating Sack of Liège in 1468.", "His episcopacy significantly weakened the autonomy of the Prince-Bishopric of Liège, making it a de facto protectorate of the Duchy of Burgundy for decades.", "His assassination in 1482 plunged Liège into further instability, contributing to the region's ongoing conflicts and power struggles after the collapse of Burgundian power."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "philip-the-good", "sourceName": "Philip the Good", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-lige", "targetName": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège", "context": "Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, used his political influence to secure the appointment of his nephew, Louis de Bourbon, as Prince-Bishop of Liège in 1456."}, {"sourceSlug": "louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-lige", "sourceName": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "liege-prince-bishopric-of-liege", "targetName": "Liège, Prince-Bishopric of Liège", "context": "Louis de Bourbon's entire episcopacy and the majority of his significant political conflicts occurred within the Prince-Bishopric of Liège from 1456 to 1482."}, {"sourceSlug": "charles-the-bold", "sourceName": "Charles the Bold", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-lige", "targetName": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège", "context": "Charles the Bold collaborated with Louis de Bourbon by militarily suppressing Liègeois rebellions, most notably leading to the Sack of Liège in 1468 to restore Louis's authority."}, {"sourceSlug": "louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-lige", "sourceName": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "burgundian-wars", "targetName": "Burgundian Wars", "context": "Louis de Bourbon's controversial rule and his reliance on Burgundian military support made the Prince-Bishopric of Liège a key theater in the broader Burgundian Wars (1474-1477)."}, {"sourceSlug": "william-de-la-marck", "sourceName": "William de La Marck", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-lige", "targetName": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège", "context": "William de La Marck, known as the 'Wild Boar of the Ardennes,' orchestrated the assassination of Louis de Bourbon on September 8, 1482."}], "places": [{"name": "Liège, Prince-Bishopric of Liège", "role": "Ecclesiastical See & Conflict Zone"}, {"name": "Dijon, Duchy of Burgundy", "role": "Center of Burgundian Influence"}, {"name": "Tongeren, Prince-Bishopric of Liège", "role": "Site of Battle & Rebellion"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-liège"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1875296", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_de_Bourbon_(bishop_of_Li%C3%A8ge)"], "tags": ["France", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège is remembered as a landmark person of the Medieval era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Louis de Bourbon's episcopacy was a pivotal regional event, as his disputed rule and reliance on Burgundian power directly led to the Sack of Liège in 1468 and significantly eroded the autonomy of one of the Holy Roman Empire's most important ecclesiastical principalities, setting the stage for future regional power shifts.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T11:08:21Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32569391708", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège was a religious figures in France (1438 – 1482-09-08), recognized as a landmark figure in the Medieval era. Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities.", "newValue": "Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège (1438 – September 8, 1482), a scion of the powerful French House of Bourbon and nephew of Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, epitomized the tumultuous intersection of ecclesiastical authority and dynastic ambition in 15th-century Western Europe. His controversial appointment to the Prince-Bishopric of Liège in 1456, orchestrated by his uncle, ignited decades of conflict between the fiercely independent Liègeois citizenry and the encroaching power of the Burgundian state, fundamentally reshaping the political landscape of the Lower Countries during the late Medieval era.

His tenure was marked by incessant strife, beginning with his forced installation by Burgundian forces after the Liègeois initially rejected him in favor of John of Heinsberg. Louis's rule was characterized by his perceived absenteeism, lavish lifestyle, and reliance on Burgundian military backing, which further alienated the burghers of Liège. Major uprisings in 1465 and 1468 saw the city repeatedly revolt against his authority and Burgundian domination, leading to the brutal Sack of Liège by Charles the Bold in 1468, a devastating event that temporarily crushed the city's autonomy and secured Louis's precarious position as a Burgundian puppet.

Despite repeated exiles and restorations, often facilitated by external powers like France and the Holy Roman Empire, Louis de Bourbon's episcopacy irrevocably weakened the traditional independence of the Prince-Bishopric of Liège. His entanglement with the Burgundian dukes, Philip the Good and Charles the Bold, made Liège a critical battleground in the broader Burgundian Wars, ultimately contributing to the region's absorption into larger political entities. His violent assassination in 1482 by William de La Marck, the 'Wild Boar of the Ardennes,' brought a bloody end to a life defined by political intrigue and popular resistance, leaving Liège in a state of profound instability."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-22T11:08:21Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32569391708", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Educated at in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Influenced by in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Born into in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}], "newValue": ["The political ambition of Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, to expand Burgundian influence into the Prince-Bishopric of Liège directly led to Louis's controversial appointment.", "Louis de Bourbon's prestigious noble birth and familial ties to the French royal house and the House of Burgundy provided the necessary leverage for his ecclesiastical advancement.", "Pre-existing tensions between the independent-minded citizenry of Liège and the traditional authority of their Prince-Bishops created fertile ground for conflict upon Louis's imposed rule."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-22T11:08:21Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32569391708", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Founded through Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Wrote through Louis de Bourbon, Bishop of Liège's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title":